{
  "requirement": "`habeas::validate_repository(root)` MUST return a report with the number of discovered requirements, the number of evidence files, and all validation errors for the supplied project. Ordinary invalid evidence MUST be reported in that report; an inaccessible project root MUST return an I/O error.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: `requirements.integration.report_api` is satisfied. The implementation is complete and the tests cover the success and failure cases.**\n\n**Implementation**\n- `habeas::validate_repository(root)` is re-exported from `src/lib.rs`. It is defined in `src/evidence.rs:119` as `Ok(inspect_repository(root)?.report)`, and carries the `{impl}` marker.\n- `EvidenceReport` (`src/evidence.rs:16`, marked `{impl}`) has `requirements`, `examples`, `evidence_files` and `errors`.\n- `inspect_repository_with_progress` (marked `{impl}`) fills these in:\n  - `requirements` counts definitions of kind `Requirement`.\n  - `evidence_files` is `evidence_files.len()`, taken from the JSON files discovered under `evidence/`.\n  - `errors` collects every problem found: configuration, definition, evidence-file, orphan, missing-evidence, reference, citation-review and Rust-coverage errors. These are all pushed into one `Vec<String>`, and none abort the run.\n- The first fallible step is `fs::canonicalize(root)?`, which returns an `io::Error` for an inaccessible or missing root. Everything after it, including a malformed `habeas.toml` or `book.toml` and invalid JSON, is pushed into `errors`.\n- This matches both halves of the requirement: ordinary invalid evidence goes into the report, and an inaccessible root is an I/O error.\n- The `examples` field is an extra count that the requirement does not mention. It is harmless, and the requirement does not forbid it.\n- The marking is complete. I found no other code that computes or returns this report without the `{impl}` marker.\n\n**Tests**\n- `validates_full_names_requirement_text_and_input_lines` asserts the `requirements` and `evidence_files` counts and an empty `errors` list.\n- `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` checks that many kinds of invalid evidence all land in the report together: missing, orphan, invalid JSON, bad schema, a false determination, changed text, bad inputs, and a duplicate or empty definition.\n- `configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks` shows that a bad `habeas.toml` or `book.toml` is reported without stopping later checks. It carries `{test}` markers for other requirements and none for this one, so I read it only as supporting evidence.\n- `inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase` checks that the report is identical with and without progress reporting, on both a valid and an invalid project.\n- `inaccessible_project_root_is_an_io_error_not_a_report` (`tests/evidence_boundaries.rs:568`) asserts that a missing root returns `Err` with `ErrorKind::NotFound`. It also asserts that invalid evidence in an accessible root appears in the report.\n- `discovered_external_cargo_outputs_validate_freshness_and_obey_disallow` and `fixme_fixture` use the public API end to end, with fresh, stale, disallowed and FIXME cases.\n\nThe tests cover both halves of the requirement, the I/O error and the in-report errors, and include boundary and failure cases.",
  "references": "b29e530bf260b1400a1b11fe3542a589c48af5d5952ad5be6bfe6199326d9394",
